{"name":"Clay Pot","hex":"#c3663f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"clay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral substance made up of small crystals of silica and alumina, that is ductile when moist; the material of pre-fired ceramics.","An earth material with ductile qualities."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add clay to or to spread clay onto.","To purify using clay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/clay"},{"term":"pot","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Potawatomi."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flat-bottomed vessel (usually metal) used for cooking food, possibly excluding saucepans (see usage notes).","The nominal household cooking vessel, metaphorically standing for the supply of food for a meal, or for the home."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pot"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pottery","blocks":[{"text":"Pottery is the process and the products of forming vessels and other objects with clay and other raw materials, which are fired at high temperatures to give them a hard and durable form. The place where a potter makes such wares is also called a pottery. The definition of pottery, used by the ASTM International, is \"all fired ceramic wares that contain clay when formed, except technical, structural, and refractory products\". End applications include tableware, decorative ware, sanitary ware, and in technology and industry such as electrical insulators and laboratory ware. In art history and archaeology, especially of ancient and prehistoric periods, pottery often means only vessels, and sculpted figurines of the same material are called terracottas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Traditional_pottery_in_Nigeria_%28Ikpu_ite%29_19.jpg/330px-Traditional_pottery_in_Nigeria_%28Ikpu_ite%29_19.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pottery","wikidata":"Q11642"},{"title":"Clay","blocks":[{"text":"Clay is a type of fine-grained natural soil material containing clay minerals. Most pure clay minerals are white or light-coloured, but natural clays show a variety of colours from impurities, such as a reddish or brownish colour from small amounts of iron oxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg/330px-Boulder_clay_cliff_south_of_Withernsea_%28geograph_6054962%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clay","wikidata":"Q42302"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Table_Service_MET_DT11573_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An 18th-century Chinese export porcelain service, for the America market","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATable_Service_MET_DT11573_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Niger%2C_Boubon_%281%29%2C_pottery_market.jpg/500px-Niger%2C_Boubon_%281%29%2C_pottery_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Niger%2C_Boubon_%281%29%2C_pottery_market.jpg/1280px-Niger%2C_Boubon_%281%29%2C_pottery_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2